---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Penguin-Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../../components/Navbar.astro';

const currentDate = new Date();
let currentYear = currentDate.getFullYear();
---

<Layout title="Penguins">
    <head>
        <meta charset="UTF-8">
        <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
        <title>Humboldt Penguin</title>
    </head>
    <body>
        <header class="header">
            <h1>Humboldt Penguin</h1>
        </header>
        <Navbar/>
        <main>
            <div class="container">
                <nav class="contents">
                    <h2>Contents:</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li><a href="#overview">Overview</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#diet">Diet</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#behavior">Social Behavior</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#facts">Facts</a></li>
                    </ul>
                </nav>

                <section id="overview">
                    <h2>Overview</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Scientific Name: Spheniscus humboldti</li>
                        <li>Size: Approximately 65-70 cm (26-28 inches) tall, weighing 3.5-5 kg (7.7-11 lbs).</li>
                        <li>Habitat: Found along the Pacific coast of South America, primarily in Peru and Chile, where the cold Humboldt Current flows.</li>
                        <li>Conservation Status: Vulnerable</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
                <section id="diet">
                    <h2>Diet</h2>
                    <p>The Humboldt Penguin's diet mainly consists of small schooling fish, such as anchovies and silversides, as well as crustaceans like krill and shrimp. They forage in the cold, nutrient-rich waters of the Humboldt Current.</p>
                </section>
                <section id="behavior">
                    <h2>Social Behavior</h2>
                    <p>Humboldt Penguins live in colonies, often nesting in burrows dug into guano deposits or in natural caves and crevices along the rocky coasts. They form monogamous pairs that may stay together for many breeding seasons. Communication involves vocalizations and visual displays. They can breed year-round, depending on food availability and environmental conditions.</p>
                </section>
                <section id="facts">
                    <h2>Interesting Facts</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>They have distinctive black and white plumage with a black band across their chest.</li>
                        <li>Humboldt Penguins are adapted to warmer temperatures compared to many other penguin species.</li>
                        <li>Their breeding success is closely linked to the availability of their prey, which can be affected by El Niño events and overfishing.</li>
                        <li>They play an important role in the coastal ecosystems of Peru and Chile.</li>
                        <li>Conservation efforts are underway to protect their nesting sites and ensure sustainable fishing practices in their foraging areas.</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
            </div>
            <footer>
                <p><span class="copyleft">&copy;</span> PengiCoder & MrPiggy105, { currentYear }</p>
            </footer>
        </main>
    </body>
</Layout>

<style>
    .contents ul {
        list-style-type: none;
        padding: 0;
    }

    .contents li {
        margin-bottom: 10px;
    }

    section {
        overflow: hidden;
        margin-bottom: 20px;
        margin-left: 30%;
    }

    h1{
        text-align: center;
        height: 100%;
        font-family: 'Orkney', Arial;
    }
</style>